WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
Formula One, built to win, exciting races all over the world.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:29.540
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.860
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:40.280
And what we have here, not just a Formula One game, probably like the earliest iteration

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:48.440
I can imagine on the Gran Turismo style structure of like a racing game that offers car purchasing,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.820
upgrading, and license testing nearly a decade before Gran Turismo 1 would go on to kind of

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.180
not only invent that style, but perfect it.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.440
So we have it here on this NES exclusive racer.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.220
This didn't come to any other platforms by the developer Seda.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:16.680
Sean, before I get too in the weeds here, just want to hear your thoughts in general on not necessarily racing games or Gran Turismo,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:26.960
but just this format specifically of kind of like working your way through like, you know, a beater car and eventually getting to the Formula One championships.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:35.740
Well, I guess like what you're describing is, I guess the term like Gran Turismo game is a bit of a misnomer.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.440
Like these are all built to win likes that we've been playing.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.520
But I really, I really like this style.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:58.240
I guess it's not so much like racing specifically that, that has gotten me into them or why I've always had a soft spot for these.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:05.000
But I think it's more so just like having an evolving shift in scope over the course of a game.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:13.480
And I think Gran Turismo 2 on PlayStation was the first time that I really felt that.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.580
And I had no idea that something like this existed on the NES.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:28.560
Yeah, it's funny because like obviously this doesn't have the car library that the Gran Turismo games have.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.360
And Gran Turismo 2 specifically just had like what seemed like an impossible number of cars to choose from.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.360
And yeah, some of them are just like slight variations.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.780
They weren't obviously like all, you know, like based on real world physics or anything.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:54.200
But it was still there and there was the opportunity to like for something I didn't know, an itch I needed scratched to like, yeah, I would like to race in a Honda Civic, you know, like everybody's trying to get you to race.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:55.800
Or a Model T. Right, right, exactly.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.600
Everybody's trying to get you to race in these like ultra fast cars.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.220
And it turns out it's like, is there a league for PT Cruisers?

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:03.440
Like maybe, maybe.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.500
And it exists right here.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:20.740
I think PT Cruisers were introduced in Gran Turismo 4, but still the idea that like every car can be raced in and that they're all like different classes and different types of races is, you know, is a welcome take.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.680
I like what you said about too, just like that the scope kind of changes.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:34.540
That's definitely true in this game too, as, you know, the cars, they upgrade from the, what is it, like a Mini Cooper, right?

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.480
Is your default car?

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.020
Yeah, Mini Cooper.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:41.620
And you're just sort of, you're jumping from city to city, finding parts.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.140
And then by the end of it, you're, you're on like, you're on the actual like Formula One circuit.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.160
So that's just cool.

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:50.980
Right. Realistic?

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.040
Maybe not. But nevertheless, you know, it's got the makings of a good movie.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.820
It's as realistic as the map of the United States that they feature in this game.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:12.780
And since I said the words movie, I'd be remiss to not mention that there was a Gran Turismo movie that like, I laughed at the very idea of existing, but apparently it's like, it's not good, but apparently it's passable.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.480
So maybe one day.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.740
Yeah, I didn't have any interest in watching that.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:27.540
So yeah, the interesting thing is like Gran Turismo as a, as a brand, I think lives in everyone's head as like an amazing, like, you know, sim racing thing.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.400
But it's like, it's leading in nothing right now.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.960
Like, it's not leading in, in like, in ridership, you know, like players, like, it doesn't have the most players.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.300
You know what I mean?

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.160
Like, no one's competing in it.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.800
Yeah, no one's competing in it.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:54.520
No one. It's not particularly well liked because they wound up like forcing a credit system that basically was like, you can try and earn all these credits, but you'd be much better off going to the PlayStation Network store.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:02.840
Mwahaha. It's like, it, they've made a lot of bad decisions from the days of like, everything's on the disc and you can have it when you want it.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:08.620
You know, I feel like it peaked with a spec or three a spec and then maybe a little bit of four.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.940
Um, and since then, Forza's had their number.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.940
Yeah, but Forza even had a tough time with the most recent one.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:24.140
I think that there were like large layoffs recently on the Forza team because of the latest, the sales of the latest just don't do it.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:36.520
But I think that maybe we should mention that's because of a little game called iRacing, which apparently like even real Formula One racers are participating in on their off season and driving on.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.100
So it's like, it's kind of hard.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.840
I know nothing about this at all.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.820
Yeah. I'm just saying it's like, it's interesting.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.160
It has nothing to do with this game even.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.200
So I'm going to wrap it up.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:51.000
But I just think it's interesting that the format that we're describing here is just surprising to see this early.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.000
I definitely didn't expect that we could get this kind of game on the NES.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.880
It is obviously different than Gran Turismo.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:10.660
But even the idea of these Gran Turismo games has changed quite a bit where Gran Turismo 7 doesn't quite have the same system that we're describing here or in the original Gran Turismo games.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.840
So it's kind of like, it's kind of lost.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:17.320
It came, it went, and I'm surprised to see it again so early here.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:25.820
So now that we've talked about the formula, the basic loop, if you will, is that you start in this clunky Mini Cooper.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:46.800
You grind through some pretty easy local races in New York, Detroit, and Miami, slowly accumulating some cash, using that cash to upgrade the parts for your car, to be able to race in the harder races in Yellowstone or Las Vegas or Denver.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:07:00.780
And then you pass the test to unlock new race tiers with a new car and eventually continue to upgrade that car until you unlock the F1 races.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:07.320
So the whole way you're just, you know, you're participating in races that you can participate in over and over again, too.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:13.600
This isn't like a level-based thing where it like tells you, okay, you start in New York level one and you got to do this race.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.440
Yeah. And then level two is this.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:16.940
It's more sandboxy. Right. Much more sandbox.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.680
You can go visit these towns as you like.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:22.520
The different towns or cities, I guess, it's not that small.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:27.760
The different cities have different shops even of like what parts are available and such.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:37.240
And the most cutting edge feature is the ability to fly out to Las Vegas, take your winnings, and just bet them right there on the slot machines, baby.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:49.380
I did do that pretty early on and had way more money than I had that I could do anything with because they wouldn't sell to me because I wasn't a good enough racer.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:56.140
So I guess it's sort of like a shortcut that may be useless for a while.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:03.140
Yes. And I think it's funny because I feel like the casino could have been a lot more punishing.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.640
Obviously, if you're emulating the game, you can just rewind if you don't like the result.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.740
And it's one of those interesting games where, you know, save states, whatever.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:16.340
It's one of those interesting games where like the result isn't necessarily predetermined.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:19.940
It's based on the frame you pull down the lever.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:21.880
So you don't have to like.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.460
So you basically, you cheated the whole time.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:25.860
No, no, no, no. I did not.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.460
Because again, to your point, it doesn't matter, right?

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:33.640
Like if you haven't done any of the rank D races, you can't go play in rank C.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:34.960
So what's the point? That's true.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:43.400
And so all that to say that the casino is there and it's definitely a way to help with grinding.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:49.140
But it's not necessarily the cheat it's made out to be, at least early on.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.640
You still have to go through all the races.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:56.340
And I think it's nice that like there's so many from the beginning.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:59.300
There's New York, Detroit, and Miami that you can go to.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.060
But there's also multiple races in each of those cities.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:14.500
The New York race where your icon or cursor starts anyway does have the $0 entry fee race to reward you with a first place $500 finish.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:20.500
And then, you know, eventually with any other race after that race, you'll have to pay to get in.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.360
But the rewards will be bigger, so on and so forth.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:30.360
But it's not until you enter the race that you really get a sense of like how your car stacks up against the other cars.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.640
So I think it's worth talking about because another, just to go back to the Gran Turismo thing,

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.940
a huge issue in those games is that money does become pointless.

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:47.040
Not right away, but like after a few hours of playtime, money becomes pointless.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:55.600
And you can essentially just save up enough money to upgrade the car so that your car is, you know, is the car for that race.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:01.800
The other cars in the race don't get like the tune-ups that your car got to be competitive against you.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:05.900
You're just now like able to go 20 to 30 miles faster than them.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.840
And of course, that's going to mean that you win the race.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:14.620
It's like, duh, nobody can outrun an accelerator that can do an extra 30 miles per hour.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:19.400
As long as you know what you're doing, you know, there's a lot of people that just go max speed no matter what.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:22.280
Like I did when I first got Gran Turismo 2.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.780
Yeah, as a kid, I just didn't even know about brakes.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.180
I just thought you just went full speed.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.940
Brakes are for suckers. Yeah, you just turned at the appropriate moment and the car would turn like any other arcade racer.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.240
But, you know, here it's not as sim-like in the racing department.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:42.700
And we can talk about that in a lot of different ways.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:49.220
But I just want to first clarify that, like, this isn't the kind of game where you have, you know, manual transmission.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:53.980
And you have to, you know, follow a driving line.

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.180
And you have to brake appropriately.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.200
It's much more arcade-y than that.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.680
Probably necessary.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:03.140
But, Sean, was that a surprise to you?

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:13.200
No, this, I mean, I just, I don't think that the tech is there for all of this to really make a lot of sense.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:17.460
Like, you're always going to be on rails in these racing games.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:24.040
But I think they did a pretty good job of making the upgrades feel like upgrades.

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:26.660
And having pros and cons.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:31.620
Like, you'll be a little bit harder to get knocked around.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:34.180
But you're going to lose some max speed.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:41.980
And, like, when you do end up getting, like, an engine upgrade, it does feel like you are genuinely going faster.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:49.640
And your handling will improve if you've got better tires on.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:12:00.340
And, like, in other games, like, where there are several different vehicles, it was a lot less obvious to me, like, what was changing.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.980
I was still like in a lot of sports games.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:05.820
Like, oh, this team's better at this and that.

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.140
And the other thing is like, well, it all felt like the same game to me.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:13.940
At least in this game, the smaller improvements are still very noticeable.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:30.160
But the whole, like, racing engine, I guess, happening here, it's impossible to make it feel like it's a real, like, it's happening in a real, like, environment.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:33.420
It's always going to feel, like, kind of outrun-like.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:34.740
Yes, yeah.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:40.360
Now, we did have Al Unser Jr.'s Turbo Racing, I think was the name of the game.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:46.220
And that game did have a little bit more, like, again, nothing like what we would see with Gran Turismo and onwards.

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:53.420
But it did have a little more, like, sim-ish nature to it, but not in the sense of, you know, like, completely unique cars.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:56.960
You know, and I get what you're suggesting here, too, about, like, even just the parts.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:05.720
You wouldn't be able to necessarily change, like, you know, tire wear and grip and handling and all that.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:07.420
That's just hard stuff to do.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:09.160
And I agree with you that it's noticed here.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:15.260
The other reason why this isn't a sim, though, by any stretch of the imagination, and this one confuses me.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:20.840
Like, I get, and I kind of appreciate it being an arcade racer with some sim-like components.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.760
But the traffic in a race, like, what is it?

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.400
Are these non-sanctioned races?

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:28.760
They have prize pools.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:29.840
They have entry fees.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.740
But, like, they didn't buy the permit for the track?

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:33.920
Like, what the hell is going on?

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:42.280
Why are there non-racing cars that are just trying to get to work on their commute and you can bump into them instead?

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:45.840
This is the Need for Speed underground universe, actually.

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:49.000
So I think that's where this is coming from.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:54.640
Or maybe it's all just different legs of the cannonball run before you join F1.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:55.860
I don't know.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:57.200
I didn't read the deep lore.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:01.680
Yeah. And so there are blue cars and there are green cars.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:04.700
The blue cars are your actual, like, opponents.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:06.220
And they're aggressive.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:12.040
They're aggressive. But I also, don't you think that the green cars, I don't think they're as aggressive, right?

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:18.420
But if I saw somebody, like, speeding, I'm not going to be like, well, I'll show them and try to, like, get them to ram into me.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:24.440
Like, I feel like they do a little bit of, like, not necessarily letting you through.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:26.720
I think, like, no.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:27.800
I'd be getting out of the way.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.580
They definitely are going way too fast.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.880
They are going as fast as people on the New Jersey Turnpike.

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:37.740
And people on the New Jersey Turnpike are insane.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:41.700
So the New York levels at least makes a little bit of sense.

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:46.680
But they don't try and actually block you like your opponents do.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:48.680
Like, they usually stay in their own lane.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:49.720
Yeah, it's unfortunate if they do.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:51.680
Yeah, they usually stay in their own lane.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:59.760
And it's sort of like this very lackadaisical merge if they're changing from lane to lane.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.560
Whereas the blue cars are actually trying to get in your way.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:12.800
And the one, like, very silly thing, at least in the beginning of this game, is that your vehicle is made of paper.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:17.840
And if you just sort of rear end one of these cars, you're going to spin out.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.940
There are some ways to counter that.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:25.900
There's a little bit of counter steering and you don't immediately spin out.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:29.560
But if you're new to the game, you're probably going to spin out a lot.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:35.440
Yeah, and, you know, we were talking even about how it's an arcadey racer.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:41.680
And it definitely is. But there are some, you know, turns on these tracks, especially, obviously, with the faster cars.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:47.580
That do require, you know, not only preemptive turning and such.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:49.360
But, like, yeah, definitely braking, right?

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:53.680
Yeah, you can't go at these things just holding down the accelerator the whole time.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:59.400
Speaking of the accelerator, every car also has nitro in it as well.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:01.060
And you have a boost thing.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:05.900
The boost thing is interesting because it's something that also costs money.

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:14.400
So after each race, depending on how much nitrous you used, you will have that remaining left.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:15.660
Yeah, so you have to fuel it up.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:21.280
But it's not that expensive by any means compared to the rewards of the games.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:28.540
And so it just becomes this, like, tedious, like, box that you sometimes forget to check of, like, did I fuel up the nitrous?

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.440
Nope, I didn't. Okay, I got to now, like, do this race.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.100
Yeah, it's a little silly.

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:39.080
I mean, at least it's not, like, something you can lean on.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:42.060
Like, you can't just lean on your nitro for the entire race.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:50.080
And I guess that's just maybe a reminder that this is a resource to be used sparingly.

00:16:51.560 --> 00:17:00.140
But maybe, I guess maybe it could have been refueled on its own and just charged to your account after each race to make it less tedious.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:03.840
And how about the actual, like, locations, you know?

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:10.680
So you have early on, as I mentioned, New York, and then eventually you upgrade to other races.

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:18.740
You can go to those cities before that, but, you know, eventually you upgrade to Dallas, Denver, Hawaii before you go international.

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:24.940
Are those tracks and places, like, are they, obviously they're not modeled.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:29.840
Like, the New York one isn't like you're driving on a specific highway or something like that.

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:33.840
But is it enough of, like, this is New York?

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.800
Or is it, like, this is just another racetrack somewhere in the world?

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.340
A little bit of both.

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:47.040
I was surprised by how many unique, like, skyboxes, I guess would be the word here, there were.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:51.920
Coupled with some, like, there are different road textures.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:55.880
There's, like, the night races look different than the day races.

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:58.700
And, like, there's grass.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:02.620
And then, like, you get, like, different visuals for each race.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:06.080
So I thought there'd be a lot more asset reuse there.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.960
But I don't think any of them really feel like anywhere.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:16.940
Like, it's the, there's going to be turns that all kind of look like the same.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:21.160
There is some simulation of, like, of going up and down hills.

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:23.740
But it doesn't, it never really feels like anything.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:26.940
It's just, this is where the track is taking me.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.160
But visually, it's, it looks cool.

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:29.820
It's just, this is where the track is going. So, how does that loop work out for you of, like,

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:32.500
you, you know, you run these races.

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:36.940
You figure out, like, kind of where you match up against the other drivers.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:38.560
Oh, that's another thing we should mention, too, is, like,

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:43.000
this also borrows from the, you know, newest set of Gran Turismo games.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.660
The idea that, like, every race is just a, a rolling start,

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:48.620
at least before you get to Formula One,

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:51.780
where basically, you know, you start and last,

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:53.500
and you just have to catch up to first.

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:56.500
This has been a major complaint in Gran Turismo 7,

00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:00.400
that, like, there is no such thing as a qualifying lap,

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:02.880
and you must always start in 16th place.

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.380
Yeah. This is how Ridge Racer worked, too.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.020
Like, I was always annoyed by that.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:08.900
It doesn't really make any sense,

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:12.220
because, like, a qualifying lap would still be, like,

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:15.080
it takes time, so you have to agree to do it, right?

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.980
You could just say, nah, skip the qualifying lap and just start and last,

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.000
but it also isn't necessarily, like,

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:22.820
a given that you would just have the fastest lap, right?

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.660
You might place, like, third or fourth,

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:25.540
and then that's interesting.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:30.220
I just feel like the reason the qualifying lap doesn't exist in the NES game

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:33.500
is the same reason it doesn't exist in Gran Turismo 7,

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:36.860
which is funny. It's that the AI isn't good enough to, like,

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:40.900
handle you being in first and then never seeing them again.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:45.880
I think that there, because there is no,

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:48.800
the simulation isn't happening.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:52.960
Like, it's just, like, the car doesn't spawn until you get up to it in this game.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:54.400
I don't know if it's the same reason.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:21:00.020
And they're more so just, they're just obstacles that move

00:21:00.020 --> 00:21:05.080
than actual, like, opponents that they get,

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.520
they fill in, like, a time after the fact

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:10.660
to make it look, to make it feel a little bit more realistic.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:15.640
But, yeah, it's annoying, but it's what I expect.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:20.640
Like, I wasn't expecting to see eight vehicles on the same screen

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.720
all trying to avoid each other.

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:24.200
I feel like that would have crashed the console.

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.260
But what I do like about, this is, I guess,

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:32.300
just a different topic entirely.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:38.580
Go there. What I did like about this game is just the sense of progression.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:41.940
And I know that we already kind of touched upon, like,

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:44.140
the idea of expanding scope, but,

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.360
and the fact that the upgrades feel like something.

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:55.460
But I think the little touch that I wasn't expecting and appreciated the most

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:57.520
was just that when you get a new car,

00:21:58.060 --> 00:22:00.660
your dashboard changes entirely.

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:04.480
And that was not, that was just weird.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:05.700
It was weird and cool.

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.860
And, like, you're kind of, like, moving forward.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:11.180
And it's not so much, like, going into the future.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:16.440
But, like, the dashboard of the Mini Cooper just looks kind of lame.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:19.900
And then as you get better and better cars, like,

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:23.440
it becomes more futuristic and, like, super car-y.

00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:26.900
And I don't know. I just wanted to chop that out.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:30.720
I think there's a lot of nice touches just throughout the game

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:33.520
presentationally. Like, I agree with you on those dashboards.

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:37.580
But also just even, like, changing parts when you buy parts upgrades

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:39.580
and seeing them be applied to the car.

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:43.320
In any other, like, NES game for the most part,

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:47.120
just like how it is in most, you know, RPGs on the system,

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.020
you buy it and then it's not like you see, like, you buy the sword, right?

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:53.120
Let's say in, like, a Final Fantasy game.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:54.820
They don't, like, you don't see the merchant, like,

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:57.580
hand you the sword in, like, a cool cutscene, right?

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.020
And you, like, hold it in your hands. You now just have the sword.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:02.260
Yeah, exactly. That could totally have happened here.

00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:04.720
You could have just, like, bought the engine and then, like,

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.620
even worse, you might have to, like, apply the engine.

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:08.780
But that doesn't happen here.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:12.460
Instead, it's like you watch the engine get, like, put onto your car.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:13.720
The old one gets taken out.

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:16.940
I think they even, if I recall correctly,

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:18.820
they buy it back from you, right?

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:23.160
You have to go to a different shop to sell all the gear.

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:25.300
Oh, okay, right, right. There are different shops to sell, yeah.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:27.280
But that's neat, right? They didn't have to do that either.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:32.200
So it's kind of like there's a reason to travel back to areas that you were at

00:23:32.200 --> 00:23:35.380
even though the races you completed there are long gone, right?

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:39.860
So you're not, like, done with New York and Miami once you get to rank C.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.560
It's just you're not going to be racing there unless you're, you know,

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:47.600
somehow blew all your cash at the casino.

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:50.000
At the casino? You need to kind of, like, yeah, work up again.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:55.440
And by the time you get to the Formula One races,

00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:57.860
the speed of the game has really changed.

00:23:58.280 --> 00:24:00.960
So I think there's, like, a noticeable jump throughout the game.

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:06.200
Oh, sorry. There's a noticeable progress, you know, very, very gently throughout the game.

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:13.960
But the Formula One stuff, like, that car is genuinely, like, hard to handle even in this arcade-y game just because of its speed.

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:24.580
So it does rely on, obviously it has way better handling, but it does rely on fast twitch reflexes because the pseudo 3D that it's doing

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:31.720
and the kind of tracks that are in Formula One, there's, you know, there's lots of winding turns and there's lots of sudden turns

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:35.540
and the speed is definitely being translated well here.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:36.880
So I'm still impressed.

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:47.800
You know, I think the wrong thing to do would just be have a miles per hour thing just go up without any sense of speed being added.

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:51.920
I think there is a true challenge to these Formula One tracks where it's like,

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:55.480
okay, yeah, you were good enough to get here, but are you good enough to stay here?

00:24:55.800 --> 00:25:02.580
Yeah, I noticed it because I didn't have enough time to actually play through the entire campaign.

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:12.860
So after playing through a bit of it, I just sort of went to free mode and played each different vehicle.

00:25:13.060 --> 00:25:14.980
And I all have a completely different feel.

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:21.000
Like the Mini Cooper is like, it's so funny that it just, that feels like most of the racing games that we've played

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:27.340
with just the handling what it is and the speed what it is.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:37.260
And then just the jump to the, I forget the model, but it's just sort of the, I guess the Econo sports car.

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.700
Like that, that's a huge difference.

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.900
And then the Ferrari is a huge difference.

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:46.940
And then the F1 is just, yeah, they did a good job with making them all feel like different vehicles.

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:52.740
The car, I can't, it's like the Vector or something like that?

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:54.700
I think you're right. The Vector sounds right.

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:55.900
Who makes that?

00:25:55.900 --> 00:25:57.040
I'm looking it up real fast.

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:58.180
Sorry. Oh, no.

00:25:58.240 --> 00:25:59.400
Vector is the maker.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:03.400
Vector looks like the car is maybe the W8.

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:06.240
The Vector W8 is a sports car.

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:07.380
Yeah, this looks like it.

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:10.600
It's pretty cool. I actually think it may even look cooler than the Ferrari in real life.

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:14.460
But the Ferrari is the F40 and that's pretty cool too.

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:23.100
So what are you going to do? There was also, did you notice that each car's like engine makes different sounds too?

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:27.820
So there's not like one stock car engine sound throughout the game.

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:29.480
There is one stock.

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:32.720
There is one stock tire screech sound.

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:39.580
And then if you're silly enough to put on music for any of the races like I did at the beginning,

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:43.800
you don't really hear any, you get no feedback.

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:45.960
Yeah, you can only have one or the other.

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:47.060
It's music or feedback.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.280
So I played every other race without the music.

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:54.080
I don't know if that was sort of how you did it.

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:58.180
But I'm sure it would have been a complete cacophony if I didn't do that.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:02.780
Or if they didn't do that and try to play music and sound effects at the same time.

00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:04.760
Right, right. The music's not half bad.

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:07.660
So I think I stuck with the music actually.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.860
I got what I got out of the – yeah, yeah.

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:13.200
I was happy to hear the engine sound effects and all that.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:14.360
But yeah, I didn't mind the music.

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:17.280
So let's talk about like some of the – I don't know.

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:20.160
These things could just be described as difficulty.

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:22.700
But also just the gameplay mechanics here.

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:26.840
Let's get a little in the weeds. The grind of it all, the money grind.

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:27.920
Is there one?

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:31.440
Did you feel like you were grinding too long in the Mini Cooper?

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:34.860
And I don't mean that from like a personal thing of just like I want to make sure I have enough money.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:38.400
But like does it take too long to get to the next upgrade path?

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:45.440
Is it enjoyable like the amount of time that they set you in these cars, the upgrades, upgrading the parts along the way?

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:53.500
Or does it just kind of become this sort of ever-moving barrier of like, okay, well, because the other cars are getting faster too,

00:27:53.920 --> 00:27:56.780
I'm just kind of still in the Mini Cooper and I want to get out.

00:27:58.020 --> 00:28:00.480
I think that there was a pretty good balance here.

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:10.260
I think that the – when I first booted up, the first thing I did was get a new engine because I could afford it, which leaves you with no money.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:16.380
And then doing all three of the New York races, I think you net maybe $900.

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:18.720
So you spend $2,000.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:25.420
So you spend $2,000 on the engine. You make maybe like $2,900 on the winnings.

00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:30.700
And then you have to – minus however much nitro you spent.

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:33.560
So it's a little slow going.

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:41.420
But I think if you're trying to like actually spend time with this game, like you're not going to be too annoyed with that.

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:44.560
It seemed to go at a pretty good clip.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:48.340
I feel like most importantly, the game had a battery backup.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.340
So this was a game that saved.

00:28:50.380 --> 00:28:51.860
And I feel like that's a huge deal.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:57.900
This isn't the kind of game that you have to pour a ton of time into and do every race in an afternoon.

00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:03.820
This is the kind of game where like you can do New York today, Detroit tomorrow, Miami.

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:11.640
It's like you can kind of just go through these areas ever so slowly and enjoy the game as opposed to like,

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:16.240
all right, well, I really just want to find the fastest path to the F1 car.

00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:17.460
It doesn't exist.

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:19.680
There is no true fast pass.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:26.300
There's kind of like shortcuts you can take along the way if you know which things to properly upgrade to just be fast enough.

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:27.880
But like that stuff requires time.

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:32.880
And, you know, you'd have to have at least done one kind of playthrough before that to get to that point anyway.

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:38.280
Yeah, you know, not everyone is trying to knock these out for a podcast, you know?

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:44.300
Yeah. And is there like there isn't really a part strategy, though, you know?

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:50.280
So in in the Gran Turismo games and the Forza games and any racing sim game nowadays,

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:55.960
it's not always that you just want to have the soft tires, you know, like you don't.

00:29:55.960 --> 00:30:00.840
So depending on how many laps you got to do and how much fuel you have and the weight of your car,

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:01.940
like those things matter.

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:06.040
Here it's kind of like you do just want to apply the best part.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:14.940
Yeah. And I was I had to kind of go back and forth with like, well, I know this from racing games

00:30:16.580 --> 00:30:19.840
that are more advanced and can simulate things.

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:30.020
So I didn't really I actually didn't even get to a part where I think like I would have been making those decisions.

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:35.420
But I had a feeling that it wasn't going to be quite as strategic.

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:46.680
And I mentioned the, you know, the nitro being kind of trivial.

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:49.420
Apparently, I didn't get there either.

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:52.600
But I did play in free play mode, the F1 car.

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:54.340
I didn't get there.

00:30:54.480 --> 00:31:00.820
But apparently during the F1 season, the refill cost of nitro and the, you know,

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:04.540
like how much nitro you use in the race matters significantly.

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:12.000
And so that becomes another balancing act of just like because of because of the straightaways

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:17.420
and because of, you know, the amount of nitro you have and the length of the tracks,

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:18.920
like it matters more.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:24.160
And so that would have been interesting in a season, you know, to kind of battle with that.

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:30.360
But I still feel like just having access to your other cars and access to the slot machine

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:38.280
and access to money makes it so that like you can you can trivialize the cost of anything in this game

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:39.860
just by racing enough.

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:45.540
So the Gran Turismo issue hasn't hasn't been retroactively solved in 1990.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:47.580
Yeah, yeah. And I don't know.

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:49.080
I don't know how to solve it.

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:55.660
If I'm being honest, I think I think that's a job for the people who actually get paid to do that stuff.

00:31:55.660 --> 00:32:00.360
Because while I've thought about it, I don't like any solution I've come up with,

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:06.000
because any solution I come up with usually involves like making it less fun of a game.

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:10.460
Like, isn't it just fun to just like have a lot of money and buy like all the cool parts for the car

00:32:10.460 --> 00:32:12.620
as immediately as you receive the car?

00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:17.720
Should you really have like, okay, you have a whole new bankroll for this new car,

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:19.980
like in every car has an individual bankrolls.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.040
You have to like ride races in them first.

00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:23.620
That sucks. That sounds awful.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:31.340
I think the concept of having a difficult economy should remain in the world of shit like EVE Online.

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:36.040
Like, as long as it's in like multiplayer, then I guess it makes sense.

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:42.660
But no, I like, can you think of a game that has a quote unquote, like difficult economy?

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:45.640
Well, so here's an interesting thing.

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:53.740
Not that Gran Turismo 7 is a single player, but like they have, you know, there's the used car dealership.

00:32:53.740 --> 00:32:56.320
There's the new car dealership.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:33:03.720
And then there's this new legend shop where like, you know, all of the highly sought after cars from either like, you know,

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:12.200
the 60s, the 70s, but also like, you know, some Formula One and GR cars and stuff like that.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:15.740
Those things all like live in the legends ones, like the best of the best.

00:33:15.740 --> 00:33:20.140
And those cars are like, you know, 15 million credits, let's say.

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:30.100
Okay. A race that takes about an hour to finish gives you about 800,000 credits.

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:35.140
So we're talking 16 hours to afford one car.

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:36.320
That sounds pretty painful.

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:39.040
Oh, but I'm sure that sounds like a difficult economy.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:44.880
It sounds like they also will give you the option to buy this currency, though.

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:46.420
Oh, yeah, absolutely.

00:33:46.840 --> 00:33:49.580
Yeah, no problem. You can go ahead and go for it.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:55.760
All right. But I would like an example of this before microtransactions.

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:58.520
Right, right, right. Yeah, yeah.

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:02.440
What was something that just was unobtainable?

00:34:02.600 --> 00:34:13.640
Was there anything ever that was just, wasn't there something in, I feel like there was something in Pokemon that was like 9,999 at like one of the casino things.

00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:15.400
But whatever. That's a joke question.

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:16.860
Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:18.440
And it's also like, yeah, this isn't a race.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:20.260
There's a racing thing. You can't race Pokemon.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:21.440
Why can't you race Pokemon?

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:26.540
They should. There should be like a, like a Greyhound race thing for Pokemon.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:30.200
They let us raise them, but they won't let us race them.

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:32.260
All right. I got that on my system.

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:36.120
To go back to the F1 stuff though, because I think it's important.

00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:47.640
Obviously, there is no, there is no at least New York racetrack that is located near the city as of yet.

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:54.560
Or within the city. They usually just like close, don't they just like make these races out of the city?

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:56.040
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah. Absolutely. Yeah, yeah.

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:57.320
And that's what I'm saying.

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:01.480
So like for the races before F1, they're all imaginary.

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:04.260
They're all like in almost impossible locations.

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:14.980
But the F1 ones are actually based on the 1990 and I think even like they had plans for the 1991 year already.

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:16.320
So they use some of those as well.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:22.900
And they are the actual tracks that were featured in those seasons for those specific locations too.

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:31.080
Because each one of those races, Brazil, Mexico City, USA, which at the time the USA, now we have three Formula One races.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:36.320
But at the time it was in Phoenix, which is interesting because I think, I don't think there's a Phoenix one anymore.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:38.920
Yeah, maybe it outgrew Phoenix.

